hi there steve coffin here  [Music]  uh today i am going to talk about output  talking getting to talking how do we  start talking  uh remember if you enjoy these videos uh  please subscribe you can uh click or tap  on the bell to get notifications  and if you want to learn languages the  way i do please come and join us at link  first of all too i want to say that as  long as the weather is nice i want to  continue doing these videos outside  it's a nice quality of of video  um so  i'm going to talk about getting the  output because i'm in my 90 day  challenge for arabic  and the first month ended i tweeted  about it i've added about 2 400 new  known words to my total of known words  in arabic i've done a lot of reading and  listening but i've also started speaking  more  and so i want to talk a little bit about  speaking and how it you know at least  how i you know integrate that into my  study program  in order i've always said in order to  speak well you have to speak a lot i  don't believe you have to start speaking  early or at any particular time you can  start speaking when you feel like  speaking in my own case i like to start  speaking when i have enough vocabulary  so that i can say a few things even if i  say them incorrectly  and  so that i in a situation where i can  understand what the other person is  saying  now i will put up a video of my  conversation with muhammad from egypt  i think it's about 20 minutes we're  going to put subtitles there for those  of you which is probably the majority  who don't speak arabic and you will see  that i understand him very well  i'm able to say certain things i say  some things incorrectly  he's a very good tutor because he keeps  me talking  and he then sends me a report with my  errors or you know phrases that i had  difficulty with he records these so i  listen to them  from my from experience i'll make the  same mistakes week after week but  gradually get better  but the speaking has a number of  functions first of all  you know obviously when you're just  doing input it's a bit of a lonely  activity you're connecting with  interesting content but you're not  communicating you're not connecting with  anyone and it is a wonderful feeling to  connect with another person in the  language you are learning especially if  it's not stressful you'll see from my  discussion conversation with muhammad  i'm not stressed i struggle to look for  words i know i'm saying things wrong but  it's an enjoyable experience i'm  enjoying the sort of social personal  interaction with muhammad so it kind of  gets me you know in a positive mood  about my arabic second of all it points  out where some of my gaps are things  that i'm struggling with i'm struggling  with the verbs so  with that i made a point of every time i  come across a word in link  i look the verb up  in the uh  reverso  uh conjugator dictionary  because that gives me an overview  because it's very difficult for me to  get used to the way the past tense is  structured in arabic as opposed to the  present tense  uh  i just have to get used to it i'm still  struggling so it's very useful for me  every time i come to a verb review the  different conjugations not try to  remember them but  i know that i have to work on getting my  verbs better  so speaking with the tutor kind of  points out your gaps things that you  need to work on that you need to notice  more  and this then is the third thing i  wanted to talk about  is you know sometimes people think  they're going to talk their way to  fluency i don't think you're going to  talk to talk your way to fluency i think  you have to build up your potential for  fluency through lots of listening and  reading and that's an ongoing thing even  after you start talking you have to  continue doing that broadening your  vocabulary and as we know it becomes  more and more difficult to learn words  because they're more and more they are  low frequency verbs words low frequency  words so it becomes tougher and tougher  to learn them and to remember them so  you have to do a lot of listening and  reading  but the in talking with the uh  and that activity  helps prepare you for speaking  and uh  i you'll see in my conversation with  muhammad  i i think i believe i speak better than  i did  before i started on my 90-day challenge  and  you know i was reminded of of the  importance like we think we're going to  talk our way to fluency it's a bit like  my golf i'm not going to play my way to  becoming a better golfer i've even taken  lessons which only serves to confuse me  but the other day i was on the driving  range and there's a member at our club  who was a  club champion at the club  his name is roy kang he went to some  universe i think arizona state or some  university in the states on a golf  scholarship  and just standing beside him on the  driving range kind of getting that that  sense of how he swings his natural  motion  helped me tremendously just watching him  help me more than deliberate instruction  and you know i am i i don't know if  there's any  you know relationship to this whole  mirror neurons thing where the same part  of our brain is activated when we hear  music  as would be activated if we played that  music let's say on a piano so there is a  lot of sort of things that we learn to  do  that come from our observing them or  hearing them  that not everything necessarily needs to  be learning by doing or learning by  having it explained there's also a lot  of learning through absorbing through  getting the rhythm of something and if  you have a rhythm to the language  through a lot of listening that helps  you when you start to speak but of  course eventually as i've always said to  speak well you have to speak a lot  because you have to also practice  retrieving what you have learned  activating your vocabulary noticing your  mistakes so that you can then  double down  in your listening and reading to notice  more things and then you bring it back  to your conversation so  with that i wanted to talk about getting  to speaking uh have a look at the  interview with with mohammed  i'm going to do another one in a month  and my main activity of course is  listening and reading but increasingly  i'm going to be speaking  uh  and  hopefully uh i will improve but my main  goal again  is is is not necessarily the speaking i  would like to see my speaking ability  improve but i want to understand these  podcasts from al jazeera because if i  can just pick them up in the morning and  listen to them and understand 70 or 80  percent i know that is going to  influence my speaking ability it's going  to make my  my uh use of the language more natural  so that then i have this this base this  potential that i can really develop in  my in my interactions uh with with  muhammad because after all it's only  going to be a few hours a week so it's  not as if i'm living in egypt and can  speak every day for hours so there you  have it  my take on getting to speaking look  forward to your comments bye for now  [Music]

